description:
Summary: This PR adds a small runtime subsystem — the 'progress guardian' — that separates the NCCL/collective handshake and progress responsibilities from the compute-launch path and makes collective submission preemption-aware. Motivation: we observed intermittent tail latency spikes under multi-tenant preemption and scheduler-induced GPU context switches. These spikes manifested as stalled collective handshakes that blocked downstream microbatch kernel launches and prevented effective KV cache reuse. Fix: (1) introduce a lightweight handshake token (progress token) that moves NCCL initial handshake and required progress work to a dedicated progress worker thread per device group, (2) make collective submission idempotent with a short-lived preemption token so the compute submitter can back out without leaving half-open NCCL state, (3) add a guarded fastpath for tiny microbatches that prefers host-vectorized allreduce with reduced synchronization when topology indicates low benefit from NCCL path, (4) add exponential backoff + watchdog for long-running collectives and graceful fallback to alternate ring-group when necessary. The description below is a single continuous string but contains the main sections implemented. Design notes: - Progress guardian: a small event-driven loop that runs progress and initial handshakes on a pinned CPU core, uses per-group completion queues. This prevents the compute hot path from blocking on kernel-level progress steps and reduces lock contention in the TP scheduler. - Preemption token: a monotonic submission token returned to compute callers; if the token is invalidated (preemption or context switch) the caller abandons the compute launch and lets the progress worker either retry or rollback the handshake cleanly. - Fastpath for tiny microbatches: when microbatch_size < config.runtime.tp.small_mb_threshold and all hosts are local, use the lightweight host-assisted reducer to reduce CUDA stream stalls. - Watchdog/backoff: if a collective handshake exceeds config.runtime.tp.collective_watchdog_ms, we try a conservative resubmit after a jittered backoff, or switch to a slower but safer ring-group via NCCL group semantics. What changed (high level): - runtime/tensor_parallel/progress_guardian.cpp: new event loop, handshake state machine, metrics hooks - runtime/tensor_parallel/progress_guardian.h: interfaces and token API - runtime/tensor_parallel/comm_scheduler.cpp: decouple handshake/submit, use token API, update retry semantics - runtime/cuda/async_launcher.cu: handle abandoned preemption token and avoid zombie kernels - runtime/cache/kv_cache_manager.cpp: updated eviction policy to account for preempted microbatch traces - tools/perf/tp_latency_bench.py: new microbench scenarios to reproduce preemption stalls - docs/ops/preemption-handshake.md: operational guidance and tuning knobs - ci/perf/checks.yaml: add a perf-regression job for TP handshake latency Tests & benchmarks: - unit: added tokens and handshake-state unit tests in runtime/tensor_parallel/tests - integration: added tp_preemption_stall_repro in testmatrix running on a 4xA100 simulated preemption scenario - perf: bench results attached in the PR show median tail improvement between 18-28% across representative models (gpt-2 small, gpt-j-6B) on 4x and 8x topologies. Commits (summary): - 1a2b3c4: initial progress_guardian skeleton and token API - 2b3c4d5: scheduler plumbing and token checks in compute submit path - 3c4d5e6: watchdog/backoff and alternate ring fallback - 4d5e6f7: small-microbatch host-reducer fastpath and perf tests - 5e6f7g8: docs, CI perf job and test fixes Changed files (high level list): - runtime/tensor_parallel/progress_guardian.cpp (.h) - runtime/tensor_parallel/comm_scheduler.cpp - runtime/tensor_parallel/tests/* - runtime/cuda/async_launcher.cu - runtime/cache/kv_cache_manager.cpp - docs/ops/preemption-handshake.md - tools/perf/tp_latency_bench.py - ci/perf/checks.yaml Minimal illustrative snippet (for reviewer context): "// token check in compute submit
if (!submission_token.is_valid()) {
  // bail out fast; progress worker will clean up/ retry
  return SubmissionStatus::Preempted;
}
// otherwise proceed to queue compute kernel
queue_kernel(...);" Backwards compatibility: - No public API changes. All changes are internal runtime-only and surfaced knobs are opt-in (defaults unchanged). Why this approach: - Lock-free progress decoupling reduces contention and allows the compute path to proceed (or back out) predictably. - Preemption tokens provide a safe, auditable way to abandon partially-started collectives without risking NCCL deadlock. - Fastpath for tiny microbatches recovers a significant portion of small-batch throughput previously lost to synchronization overhead. Review conversation (high level): - Marcus Chen: "Can the progress guardian saturate a single CPU core under heavy multi-tenant load?" - Sofia (author): "We pin the progress loop and keep its work bounded; heavy scenarios were tested and CPU usage stayed within a single core with <5% system overhead on our perf rigs. Added config.runtime.tp.progress_worker_cpu_limit for operators." - Ravi Kapoor: "How do we guarantee we don't leave a dangling NCCL communicator when the token is invalidated?" - Sofia: "Communicator lifecycle is handled by the progress guardian; we added explicit rollback code paths that call ncclCommAbort() + a compact retry policy. See progress_guardian.cpp:rollback_path() for details." - Elena Park: "Can we get a smoke test in CI that simulates preemption?" - Sofia: "Added tp_preemption_stall_repro to integration matrix; it runs under a lightweight container that simulates context switch via SIGSTOP/SIGCONT on GPU processes." CI / Checks: - build: passed - unit-tests: passed - integration-tests: passed (tp_preemption_stall_repro: pass) - perf-regression: pass (no regression) - lint: passed Risk & mitigation: - Risk: slightly increased CPU usage for progress workers under massive scale. Mitigation: configurable CPU cap, and guardian is disabled for dedicated pools by default in release rollouts. - Risk: complexity around rollback semantics. Mitigation: conservative default retry policy and aggressive telemetry to detect stuck handshakes (new metrics: tp.handshake.latency_ms, tp.handshake.failures). Rollout plan: - Canary on Dedicated 1 (low load) for 48 hours - Canary on a single-host TP cluster with simulated preemption - Ramp to default after 7 days if no regressions observed Documentation: docs/ops/preemption-handshake.md contains operator knobs and recommended tuning values. Related work / references: - Related ENG-8421 (TP backpressure work) - Prior PRs: small-microbatch fastpaths inspired by experimental host-reducer - Notes: This PR intentionally avoids changing kernel implementations besides async_launcher entry checks; focused on protocol & scheduler-level fixes.
